### 🔴 CRITICAL — Sensitive Data Exposure via Hardcoded PII (OWASP A02)

**Finding:** User data (names, emails) is hardcoded directly in the route handler. In any real evolution of this code, real PII would likely end up hardcoded or checked into source control.

**Risk:** PII in source code leaks via version control history, logs, and error messages.

**Fix:** Never hardcode user data. Always source it from a database with parameterized queries:

```js
const { Pool } = require('pg');
const db = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });

app.get('/users', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  try {
    // Only select the fields you actually need
    const { rows } = await db.query('SELECT id, name, email FROM users');
    res.json(rows);
  } catch (err) {
    // Never leak DB error details to the client
    console.error('DB error:', err);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
  }
});
```

---

### 🔴 CRITICAL — No Input Parsing Middleware / Injection Surface (OWASP A03)

**Finding:** There is no `express.json()` or `express.urlencoded()` middleware, and no input validation anywhere. When POST/PUT routes are added (as they inevitably will be), there is no framework preventing injection payloads from reaching business logic.

**Risk:** SQL injection, NoSQL injection, and command injection become trivially easy to introduce as the codebase grows.

**Fix:** Add body parsing with strict size limits AND a validation library from day one:

```js
const { body, param, query, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

// Strict body parsing with size cap to prevent DoS
app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false, limit: '10kb' }));

// Validation middleware factory
function validate(rules) {
  return async (req, res, next) => {
    await Promise.all(rules.map(rule => rule.run(req)));
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }
    next();
  };
}

// Example: validated POST route
app.post('/users',
  requireAuth,
  validate([
    body('name').isString().trim().isLength({ min: 1, max: 100 }).escape(),
    body('email').isEmail().normalizeEmail(),
  ]),
  async (req, res) => {
    // req.body is now safe to use
  }
);
```

